Highlights
1. Groundwater shows spatial variation in physicochemical and trace element levels.
2. Rock-water interaction and arid climate likely influence groundwater composition.
3. Contaminant transfer pathways are linked to lithology and land–water processes.
Keywords Arsenic contamination, Sulphides, Rock-water interaction, Groundwater
hydrogeochemistry, Geogenic sources, Tosham ring complex
1 Introduction
Water authorities are exploring effective and efficient mitigation strategies for access of
sustainable drinking water sources to meet increasing clean water demands for human
needs worldwide [1–3]. However, significant constraints exist that limit the access to
safe and clean drinking water, which consequently affect approximately 2.2 billion individuals globally [3, 4]. One of the contributing factors towards significant constraints
is widespread geogenic contaminants and emerging pollutants that are contributing to
the global depletion of clean water sources [5]. Consequently, the progress toward the
United Nations Sustainable Development Goal of ensuring access to clean water for
drinking and other human uses by 2030 is obstructing [3, 4].
Groundwater is a viable option for various household, industrial, and irrigation purposes [5–7]. On a global scale, a large proportion of the population is dependent on
groundwater (e.g., ~ 50% for drinking, ~ 30% for agricultural purposes) [8, 9]. Given the
higher dependency on groundwater resources for different needs, the quantity and quality of groundwater aquifers changes [10–12]. In the context of global environment and
climate changes, the changes in groundwater quality represent a momentous issue [13].
Among the various groundwater quality concerns, the release of heavy metals has had a
significant impact on groundwater quality [14–16].
Arsenic has proven to be a serious threat for human health [17] and may be transported to soil, water, and air through various geogenic (e.g., geological formations, volcanic activities) and anthropogenic processes (e.g., mining activities) [18–21]. Arsenic
is present in both inorganic As(III) or As(V) species and organic As (e.g. monomethylarsonic acid, dimethylarsinic acid, trimethylarsine oxide, arsenobetaine, and arsenocholine), while inorganic species are more toxic than organic compounds [22, 23]. The
occurrence of arsenic in aquifers is largely controlled by geological, soil chemical, hydrogeological, and geomorphological factors [23–30]. The enrichment of As and its mobilization in groundwater aquifers is controlled by three geochemical processes: adsorption,
reductive dissolution of Fe/Mn hydroxides, and oxidation of sulfide in arid and semi-arid
regions [28, 30, 34, 51, 57].
Geogenic contamination of As in groundwater has been reported worldwide. According to the WHO guidelines for drinking water quality, high As concentrations (> 0.01
ppm) in groundwater have affected 500 million people of more than 100 nations across
different continents [4, 31]. The most affected continents are Asia [32–34] and Europe
[26, 35], followed by Africa [36–39], North America [40], South America [20, 41, 42]
and Australia [43]. Note that groundwater quality deterioration has impacted human life
mainly in the villages of developing nations such as India [44].
In India, more than 70% of its population relies on groundwater for their drinking,
agriculture, and other needs [7]. However, the presence of various geogenic contaminants such as As, fluoride, Fe and nitrate restrict its safe usage. Arsenic and fluoride

