Corresponding Author’s email: mmbaama@gmail.com
2

Received on: October, 2025 Revised and Accepted on: November, 2025

Published on: December, 2025

ABSTRACT
The issue of soil pollution has taken on a rather acute environmental and human health situation in agrarian
Nigerian states, especially in states like Niger where agricultural activities constitute the main source of living in
the rural areas. The penetration of heavy metals, agrochemical residues, hydrocarbons and other enduring
pollutants in the agricultural soils are very dangerous because it will be transferred to food crops and will finally
end up in the human food chain. This paper examines the levels of soil pollution, and the food-chain transfer
processes in some of the selected agricultural and mining affected communities in Niger state. Priority is given to
such contaminants as: lead (Pb), cadmium (Cd), arsenic (As) and mercury (Hg), chromium (Cr), and zinc (Zn)
which are often related to artisanal mining, excessive use of fertilizers, industrial effluents, and random dumping
of waste. Based on modern environmental-geochemical methods, the study combines the physicochemical
analysis of the soil, crop-uptake analysis, the transfer-factor analysis, exposure-risk analysis to evaluate the
magnitudes and the trophic mobility of contamination. The representative samples of soil and crops are analyzed
to identify the concentration of heavy-metals and their distribution of accumulation in soil and plants. Geospatial
methods are used to map the hot spots of contamination, test the spatial variation and find the communities that
are at a significant risk of exposure. The paper also uses human-health risk assessment indices to describe the
possible non-carcinogenic and carcinogenic risks that are related to the consumption of contaminated food crops.
Recent empirical research in Niger State shows that the soils located near the mining areas and agricultural settings
that have high agrochemical application have high levels of heavy-metals. A number of food crops are also highly
bio accumulative; especially the leafy vegetables and root tubers; their transfer-factors are higher than the advised
safety limits. The consequences of these contamination patterns are deep as they are the decrease in the quality of
crops and ecological decay, as well as long-term health hazards among the inhabitants. The study is relevant to
the current body of knowledge because it offers a multi-layered evaluation of the processes of soil contamination
and food-chain transfer in the Niger State. It highlights the dire necessity of better environmental surveillance,
better regulation of agricultural-inputs and community-based public-health based interventions. It recommends
the steps that can be used to help in evidence-based policymaking and to encourage sustainable land-use processes
to enhance the health of the soil, food safety and human wellbeing in the state of Niger.
Keywords: Soil Pollution, Heavy metals, Mining, Agriculture, Niger State
