is cultivated for its edible rosette leaves. In Iraq, 26507 tons
are produced annually on 3317 hectares of farmed land [4].
The majority of lettuce is grown in the central part of Iraq,
where seeds are sown in late summer to produce a crop
in the winter. In the northern region, the growing season
commences in late autumn, yielding a crop in the spring [5].
In Iraq, the majority of farmers utilize sewage water for the
irrigation of leafy vegetables, particularly lettuce, resulting
in the contamination of these crops and posing a health
risk to consumer communities. In the Erbil governorate
of Iraq, approximately 225 hectares are irrigated using
non-pre-treated sewage water for the cultivation of various
uncooked vegetables, which are consumed by nearly two
million people as a significant component of their diet [6].

