Simultaneous Adsorption of Chromium, Nickel, and Phenol from Landfill
Leachate Using Pineapple Residues Biochar
Abudu Ballu Duwiejuah1*, Hamdia Neena Farouk1 & Abraham Kusi Obeng1
1 University for Development Studies, P. O. Box TL 1350, Tamale, Ghana.
* Author for Correspondence ORCID ID; https:// orcid.org/0000-0002-9033-0526; Email: aduwiejuah@uds.edu.gh

Article DOI: https://doi.org/10.37284/eajenr.9.1.4449
Date Published:

ABSTRACT

03 February 2026 Toxic substances are common in landfill leachate and pose significant risks
to the environment and human health. Pineapple residue biochar was used
Keywords: to remove chromium, nickel, and phenol from landfill leachate. This study
was to evaluate the adsorption potential of pineapple residue biochar for the
Chromium, removal of chromium (Cr), nickel (Ni), and phenol from landfill leachate.
Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) and X-ray powder
Functional groups,
diffraction (XRD) analysis were used to evaluate the pineapple biochar
Landfill leachate, produced. The effects of biochar dose, exposure duration, and initial
Pineapple residue pollutant concentrations on removal efficacy were evaluated using batch
biochar, adsorption experiments. The adsorption efficiency of Cr, Ni, and phenol
Pseudo-second-order. ranged from 99.23% - 99.72%, 98.72% - 99.55%, and 23.62% - 48.03%,
respectively. The removal efficiency of greater than 99% for Cr and 98%
for Ni by the pineapple residue biochar as an adsorbent proved highly
effective. The kinetic study showed pseudo-second-order kinetics had the
best fit, with the most R2 values being > 0.90. The FTIR spectra indicate
that the pineapple residue biochar contains aromatic, hydroxyl, carbonyl,
and carboxylate groups, which were crucial for the adsorption of toxic
metals and phenol in the landfill leachate owing to their interactions. The
mineral composition of pineapple residue biochar has a significant influence
on the adsorption capacity of pollutants in the landfill leachate. The high
adsorption efficiency of the pineapple residue biochar is attributed to its
lignocellulose content, surface area, the presence of organic functional
groups, and mineral content, as regulating mechanisms for effective
contaminant adsorption. Further studies should be conducted on the
efficiency of pineapple residue biochar in removing other harmful
contaminants, emphasising organochlorines.
