INTRODUCTION
The presence and contamination of hazardous
metals in landfill leachates that end up in our
water resources have caused a great deal of
concern in recent years (Joseph et al., 2019).
Landfill leachates are complicated effluents with
a variable makeup that includes a large number of
biorecalcitrant and extremely harmful substances
(Scandelai et al., 2017). Heavy metals like Hg, Cd,
Pb, As, Cr, and phenol, which pose significant
risks to the environment and human health if
ignored, are found in landfill leachate (Hussein et
al., 2021). Chromium (Cr), cadmium (Cd), lead
(Pb), mercury (Hg), nickel (Ni), copper (Cu), zinc
(Zn), iron (Fe), and selenium (Se) are among the
metals frequently discovered in landfill leachate
(Kilgo et al., 2022). These pollutants are
commonly found in landfill leachate and, if not
treated, may pose a significant threat to human
health and the environment.
